<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Andong</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Andong is a city in North Gyeongsang province in south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-korea">South Korea</a>, on the upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nakdong-river">Nakdong River</a>. In 1361, when Red Turban rebels from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> took the Goryeo capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaesong">Kaegyong</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gongmin-of-goryeo">King Gongmin</a> took refuge there. Under Joseon it became a stronghold of Confucian scholarship, and its Hahoe Folk Village and Dosan Seowon academy are <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> World Heritage properties.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>A yangban was expected to study the Confucian classics rather than work with his hands, and many lived on country estates as teachers and local leaders, as Yi Hwang did at his academy near <b>Andong</b>.</blockquote><a href="/societies/joseon">Social Hierarchy of Joseon Korea</a></li></ul></section>
